WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:01.829
Josie is a half-cat.

00:00:01.829 --> 00:00:05.640
No, she isn’t half cat, half something else.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.790
And she’s not a humanoid with feline features
either.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:11.950
She is literally half of a cat.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:17.110
Actually, to be more precise, she’s the
front half, but an otherwise ordinary black

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:18.830
and grey tabby cat.

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:23.150
That is, ordinary apart from having no visible
back legs or hindquarters.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:27.100
You might think that’d make it hard for
Josie to get around, but if you asked her

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.200
how only being a half-cat affects her on a
daily basis…

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:35.539
well, she wouldn’t be able to answer you
due to the simple fact that cats generally

00:00:35.539 --> 00:00:36.769
can’t speak.

00:00:36.769 --> 00:00:43.149
But nevertheless, Josie will happily and effortlessly
walk around on her own two legs, moving as

00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:46.920
if she had a second pair of hind legs in place
behind her.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:52.590
Her back half isn’t invisible, it’s as
if it’s not even there at all, not showing

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.069
up on X-rays.

00:00:54.069 --> 00:01:00.699
And yet, it can give a slight yield when touched,
being almost tangible, but not fully.

00:01:00.699 --> 00:01:04.720
Despite this, Josie suffers no adverse health
problems or any other complications despite

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.060
lacking one half of her body.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:11.150
Occasionally, she doesn’t mind having her
semi-corporeal hind quarters stroked, although

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:14.800
she will usually get a little bit scratchy
if people try that.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.530
So, how does the SCP Foundation keep an anomaly
like Josie contained?

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:23.330
Well, it turns out that they don’t really
need to.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:27.730
She’s perfectly harmless and is actually
allowed to roam freely around the lower levels

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:29.750
of the facility she’s kept at.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:35.260
Casually as you like, she slinks around the
corridors, passing from one room to the next.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:40.240
Of course, if cats could talk, then Josie
would probably tell you that she has the run

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.100
of the whole facility.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:46.800
The only real question left is, what does
a half-cat do when she’s left to wander

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.180
around a Foundation facility completely unsupervised?

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:53.400
Easy, she starts looking for a ball of yarn.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.979
Josie was sure she’d seen one somewhere
around here, one of the researchers had carried

00:01:57.979 --> 00:02:00.780
it off for testing not too long ago.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:06.030
Her small front paws patted against the ground,
as Josie navigated her way to where she last

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:09.530
remembered seeing that coveted ball of yarn
being taken.

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:13.569
She passed a group of researchers, one of
them kneeling down to give her a friendly

00:02:13.569 --> 00:02:15.639
scratch behind the ears.

00:02:15.639 --> 00:02:19.450
Just as she closed her eyes and purred at
the greeting, a sound coming from down the

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:20.959
hall startled her.

00:02:20.959 --> 00:02:23.280
It was a low, distressed sobbing.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:28.579
Having never been taught that old saying about
curiosity and cats, Josie slunk past the researcher’s

00:02:28.579 --> 00:02:31.060
legs towards the source of the noise.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:36.469
The room that the crying was coming from had
no windows, no way for her to get in.

00:02:36.469 --> 00:02:42.120
Luckily, Josie – despite only having half
a body – had enough determination and resourcefulness

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.499
for two whole cats.

00:02:44.499 --> 00:02:49.400
Around the corner, she knew there was an open
air vent, and, sure enough, she leaped gracefully

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:50.909
up to it with ease.

00:02:50.909 --> 00:02:54.829
The cold metal tunnel was just big enough
to accommodate her little furry self as she

00:02:54.829 --> 00:02:58.510
crawled through, the echoing sound of sobs
guiding her.

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:03.659
As she reached the grate looking out into
the windowless room, Josie spotted a tall,

00:03:03.659 --> 00:03:06.209
thin creature sitting with its back to her.

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:10.010
Its arms would have been long enough to reach
the ground if it had been stood at its full

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:14.999
height, but for now they were wrapped tightly
around its legs as the creature rocked back

00:03:14.999 --> 00:03:16.090
and forth.

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:20.779
Its loud wails showed it was clearly in a
great deal of distress, although Josie wouldn’t

00:03:20.779 --> 00:03:25.129
be able to understand why having someone look
at its face would make this slender creature

00:03:25.129 --> 00:03:26.180
so upset.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:30.430
Nearby, there was a giant metal cube with
a hole ripped in it, and while the rest of

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:35.279
the room was empty, there seemed to be several
large, red smears on the ground.

00:03:35.279 --> 00:03:36.810
How strange.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:41.959
She cocked her head to one side and meowed,
tapping one paw against the air vent grate.

00:03:41.959 --> 00:03:48.099
It seemed pretty loose; with a few good pushes,
the screws came free and the adorable half-cat

00:03:48.099 --> 00:03:49.349
hopped down into the room.

00:03:49.349 --> 00:03:55.010
As she got closer to the creature, she noticed
it had a black bag covering its face.

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:59.510
Little did Josie know, an hour earlier, a
junior researcher had made the terrible mistake

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:04.309
of putting on a pair of x-ray glasses he ordered
from the back of a comic book.

00:04:04.309 --> 00:04:09.379
As it turns out, these were the only ones
to ever actually work: He saw right through

00:04:09.379 --> 00:04:13.739
096’s containment cube and all hell had
broken loose.

00:04:13.739 --> 00:04:15.639
But Josie didn’t mind.

00:04:15.639 --> 00:04:20.150
She decided to endear herself to the strange,
shy creature by rubbing up against its long

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:21.150
leg.

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:25.170
Returning the same affection that it had so
rarely been given, it bent down and gently

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:27.750
scooped Josie up in its spindly arms.

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:32.070
The pair of them sat in the corner together,
Josie getting herself comfortable on the tall

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:36.250
creature’s lap, purring as long fingers
ran over her tabby fur.

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:41.060
The creature, being so careful not to harm
the half cat, treated her like she was as

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:42.630
delicate as glass.

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:47.450
It had briefly stopped crying, thanks to its
visitor, and as much as she noticed the sounds

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:52.270
of sobbing had ceased, Josie would never understand
the difference she had made to SCP-096’s

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:53.270
day.

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:57.140
Unfortunately, it wasn’t long before their
encounter was cut short.

00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:02.670
A legion of guards in SCRAMBLE goggles burst
into the room to retake control of the situation.

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:08.250
The last thing they expected to see was 096
sitting calmly with Josie on its lap and the

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.060
bag covering its face.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:13.810
But the sudden entrance of a security team
startled the poor half-cat.

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:17.780
Within a second, she had bolted out of the
room, shooting under the legs of the guards

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:20.290
and back out into the facility.

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:24.990
Now that she was back outside, Josie recalled
her earlier hunt for a ball of yarn to play

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:25.990
with.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:29.850
She still wasn’t sure where it had gotten
to, maybe someone had moved it.

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:33.960
Pacing over to the elevator, she stared up
at the heavy steel doors.

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:38.300
The numbers above were gradually ticking down,
denoting which floor the elevator had currently

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:39.400
stopped at.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:44.820
Of course, Josie couldn’t read, so instead,
she sat and meowed loudly at the elevator.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:47.500
Maybe if she made enough noise the doors would
open.

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:52.370
Eventually, after a few short minutes of waiting
and growling, the doors slid apart.

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:56.840
Another gaggle of researchers passed her by,
and Josie plodded into the elevator, spotting

00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:58.530
a familiar face.

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:03.790
One of the researchers, a doctor by the name
of Sophie Cartwright, was still inside, and

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:06.300
smiled down at the half-cat explorer.

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:10.060
Doctor Cartwright had come across Josie a
few times, enough for the intrepid little

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:12.050
tabby to consider them friends.

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:13.860
“Where to, Josie?”

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:17.540
Doctor Cartwright asked, her hand hovering
over the elevator’s buttons.

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:22.300
As if in reply, Josie looked up and meowed;
humans always seemed to smile if she meowed

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:24.320
after they’d said something to her.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:26.930
Sophie chuckled and pushed the door close
button.

00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:28.210
“Hold the elevator!”

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:30.860
another, much gravellier voice called.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:36.070
A hand, or rather a paw, reached out and caught
the doors before they could slide closed.

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:41.220
It was another of the Foundation’s doctors,
one with both a considerable reputation and

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:44.930
a very… distinguishing look, to say the
least.

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:48.970
Doctor Kain Pathos Crow stepped inside the
elevator and pushed one of the buttons.

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:53.340
Sophie had to force herself to keep her head
down, but couldn’t help herself from stealing

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:58.160
the occasional glance up at Doctor Crow; she’d
heard the rumors about a peculiar anomalous

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:02.600
event changing his appearance but had never
seen him up close before.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:07.240
Josie, on the other hand, couldn’t take
her eyes off Crow, staring up at him, frozen

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:10.510
to the spot and letting out a low, cautious
whine.

00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:14.820
If her tail was visible, Sophie would have
seen it swaying from side to side, as the

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:19.940
brave little half-cat kept her defensive ground,
standing between her friend Doctor Cartwright

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:21.480
and her mortal enemy.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:23.670
You see, Doctor Crow was a dog.

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:28.620
Or at least, a man who had been turned into
something very much like a dog.

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:32.730
Still capable of speech, but with a distinctively
canine body.

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:36.980
Crow kept to himself the entire ride down,
although Sophie could feel the tension between

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:40.010
the dog-bodied doctor and her little half-cat
friend.

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:45.070
Out of the corner of his eye, Doctor Crow
spotted Josie, trying his best to ignore her.

00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:50.110
He let out a low, discomforted growl, the
kind a dog makes when they’re on edge.

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:54.889
To his credit, he couldn’t really help it,
the man had been turned into a canine after

00:07:54.889 --> 00:07:55.889
all.

00:07:55.889 --> 00:07:59.920
But it took all his stoicism to suppress a
bark and to stop himself from chasing Josie

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:02.070
around the cramped elevator.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:05.900
Arriving at her floor, Sophie stepped out,
followed by little Josie, who moved as far

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:08.280
away from Crow as quickly as she could.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:12.840
The doors closed once more and sent him further
down into the depths of the facility.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:16.830
As she turned to look, Sophie noticed Josie
had craned her head up to check on her.

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:19.139
“Aw, were you protecting me, Josie?”

00:08:19.139 --> 00:08:24.510
she asked with a smile, bending down to thank
the half-cat with a scratch behind the ears.

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:28.750
Before she could stay for too long, Josie
and her human companion parted, each heading

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:31.310
down separate winding corridors.

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:36.430
There was a funny smell hanging in the air,
like something gradually melting and bubbling.

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:40.890
Only to reform and melt again, filling the
half cat’s pink nose with a strange burning

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:41.890
scent.

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:46.230
As she passed a holding cell, something broke
Josie’s concentration yet again: the noise

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:48.550
of a hand tapping at glass.

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:53.290
She turned and instantly meowed a greeting,
to the second familiar face of the day, albeit

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:55.040
a more beaked face.

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:59.500
Kneeling down on the other side of the glass,
the black-robed Plague Doctor waved politely

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:01.930
at the dual-pawed passer-by.

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:05.470
Sitting in front of the glass, hoping to be
let in, Josie gave another meow.

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:07.790
“I’m quite well,” the Doctor mused.

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:11.000
“And how are you, my dear?” he added politely.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:12.770
Josie got up on her back legs.

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:16.840
Or rather, that’s what it would have looked
like if she had hindquarters.

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:21.120
Instead, it looked like she was almost floating
in place from halfway down, stretching her

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:26.330
front paws up, reaching towards the bird-like
face as the masked Doctor playfully tapped

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:29.430
his fingers against the glass to amuse his
feline friend.

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:35.590
“No, I can’t let you in, I’m afraid,”
he sighed, sounding a touch more melancholic;

00:09:35.590 --> 00:09:38.970
not that a half-cat can understand the difference
in someone’s cadence.

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:43.910
“Not all of us here are gifted the freedom
you enjoy, my furry friend.

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:47.160
Freedom is not something this organization
would ever understand.

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:52.580
No matter how much they claim to know of us,
the fact of the matter is, they never really

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:53.580
listen.”

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:57.290
From the other side of the glass, Josie blinked
and cocked her head to one side confused.

00:09:57.290 --> 00:09:59.640
She’d barely heard a word he had said.

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:04.010
“Oh, but listen to me go on,” the Plague
Doctor chuckled, rapping his fingers in front

00:10:04.010 --> 00:10:05.210
of the half-cat again.

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:08.920
“I suppose you’ll be looking for that
ball of yarn they took for testing.

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:12.080
I saw them carrying the box it was in that
way.”

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:16.760
He pointed, and Josie’s little green cat
eyes followed the direction of his finger.

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:20.950
Barely able to comprehend what had been said
to her, she simply meowed up in thanks and

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:23.760
plodded the way she’d been directed.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:29.060
“Farewell, little Josie,” SCP-049 said
quietly from inside its cell, watching the

00:10:29.060 --> 00:10:31.810
half-cat until she had disappeared from view.

00:10:31.810 --> 00:10:35.640
As she continued through the lower levels
of the facility, Josie slipped through doors

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:40.670
seconds before they closed, leaped up onto
tables, and dashed between legs.

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:44.990
That strange smell was back, having caught
her attention and distracted her from her

00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:46.260
quest again.

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:49.890
Tracking it to its source, Josie suddenly
felt uneasy.

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:54.580
She entered a room with a huge vat of acid,
the source of the burning scent she’d picked

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:55.580
up.

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:59.800
Though it was what was inside the vat, trapped
in a loop of regenerating itself only to be

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:04.540
melted over and over again by the acid, that
got her feeling defensive.

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:09.720
The vast, reptilian monstrosity sensed a small
life form outside its tank, and opened one

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:15.540
yellow eye, squinting through the searing
burning acid at the tiny little creature staring

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:16.540
at it.

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:20.640
The reptile snarled, screwing its now-healing
eye shut and swiping what was left of its

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:21.860
limbs at the glass.

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:27.110
Rearing back, Josie gave the monster her fiercest
hiss, baring her fangs at it.

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:31.660
This beast usually abhorred other beings,
finding all other forms of life unnatural,

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:36.720
unforgivable and so detestable that it would
normally stop at nothing to eradicate them.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:42.460
But today, a tiny tabby cat with her entire
back half missing was standing up to the crocodilian

00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:46.390
nightmare, and it barely had enough will to
retaliate.

00:11:46.390 --> 00:11:50.680
Maybe it was just lethargic, too tired to
try anything today.

00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:55.080
Perhaps it didn’t think she was worth the
effort, or figured it would see to her – along

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:59.510
with the researchers and guards – next time
it broke free from containment and went on

00:11:59.510 --> 00:12:00.870
yet another killing spree.

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:05.930
But, in Josie’s mind, this facility was
her home and the brave little half-cat had

00:12:05.930 --> 00:12:09.570
scared SCP-682 into staying put.

00:12:09.570 --> 00:12:14.680
As she trotted away again, she knew that monstrous
reptile would have to think twice before messing

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:15.740
with her.

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:21.260
Back on track, at last, Josie rounded a corner
and arrived in a familiar-looking corridor.

00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:24.740
She was certain this was where she’d seen
a researcher carrying the box containing the

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:26.560
string that she coveted.

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:31.270
Sure enough, she could see into the room where
the box had been left, noticing a group of

00:12:31.270 --> 00:12:35.080
researchers and a human in an orange jumpsuit
were gathered around it.

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:39.350
A few were making notes on their clipboards
at a safe distance, while the one in orange

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:41.250
stepped closer to the box.

00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:46.411
Josie watched as a single bee flew out of
the box, her feline eyes following it, ready

00:12:46.411 --> 00:12:48.500
to pounce if it came close enough.

00:12:48.500 --> 00:12:52.060
The man wearing the jumpsuit was waving his
arms, trying to swat the insect away.

00:12:52.060 --> 00:12:57.500
A moment later, he grabbed his arm in pain;
the bee had gotten agitated, stinging him

00:12:57.500 --> 00:13:00.020
and flying away, somehow surviving.

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:03.260
As she waited, Josie’s patience was rewarded.

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:07.149
The researchers and the man wearing orange
bustled their way out of the room, offering

00:13:07.149 --> 00:13:12.320
her the chance she needed to zip between them
and get one step closer to her prize.

00:13:12.320 --> 00:13:17.450
The room was entirely empty, save for the
box in the center, sitting precariously atop

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:18.450
a table.

00:13:18.450 --> 00:13:24.161
Wiggling herself as she readied her invisible
and intangible back legs, Josie shot up, just

00:13:24.161 --> 00:13:25.750
missing the table’s edge.

00:13:25.750 --> 00:13:29.980
She clawed and scratched at the surface, trying
to pull herself up, but her lack of hindquarters

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:32.850
made it impossible to climb the rest of the
way.

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:38.010
Dropping back to the floor, she looked up
at the table and gave a loud, indignant meow.

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:42.790
Changing tactics, she started to hop up one
of the table legs, digging her claws into

00:13:42.790 --> 00:13:45.660
the woodwork to hoist herself up bit by bit.

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:50.459
Sure enough, her new strategy worked a treat,
and Josie was presented with what she had

00:13:50.459 --> 00:13:51.890
been searching for.

00:13:51.890 --> 00:13:55.380
One final hurdle was left, however: the box
was closed.

00:13:55.380 --> 00:13:59.890
Now, the struggles of only being half a cat
are one thing, but not having opposable thumbs

00:13:59.890 --> 00:14:03.560
is a difficulty that has plagued all cats
since the dawn of time.

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:08.420
But, not being able to grab or hold things
with the ease of a human, there’s one thing

00:14:08.420 --> 00:14:11.550
cats are great at, and that’s pushing things
over.

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:15.890
Nudging the box to the table’s edge with
gentle ‘boops’ of her nose, Josie eventually

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:21.250
tipped the whole thing off, causing it to
land on its side on the floor, the lid falling

00:14:21.250 --> 00:14:22.250
open.

00:14:22.250 --> 00:14:26.330
Triumphantly, she jumped back down to claim
her prize, expecting to find the ball of yarn

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:28.570
and spend the next few hours playing with
it.

00:14:28.570 --> 00:14:33.790
But something was wrong, the yarn had changed
and now it was… looking at her.

00:14:33.790 --> 00:14:37.642
The ball had started as yarn and ribbon, the
perfect plaything for any half cat, or full

00:14:37.642 --> 00:14:39.220
cat for that matter.

00:14:39.220 --> 00:14:44.120
But the Foundation had been running their
usual invasive tests on it, and since cutting

00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:46.990
a portion of the string off, it had transformed.

00:14:46.990 --> 00:14:52.340
It was now a ball of meat, functioning, blinking
eyes staring out of a mass of overlapping

00:14:52.340 --> 00:14:54.240
sinew and blood-red matter.

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:58.130
The multiple eyes looked up at the half-cat
that had knocked it to the floor, and asked

00:14:58.130 --> 00:15:03.230
a single, simple question in a deep voice
emanating from somewhere within its lipless

00:15:03.230 --> 00:15:06.360
form:
“Are you Eric?”

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:10.100
Completely unphased by this, Josie whacked
the ball with her paw and watched as it rolled

00:15:10.100 --> 00:15:12.000
halfway across the room.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:16.080
Tentacle segments of its ‘body’ gradually
pulled the amorphous mass back to where it

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:20.580
had started, and then it asked once again:
“Are you Eric?”

00:15:20.580 --> 00:15:24.470
Once again, Josie slapped the ball and watched
as it rolled away from her, both completely

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:28.660
unaware of who ‘Eric’ was and a little
perturbed that someone had replaced the ball

00:15:28.660 --> 00:15:31.269
of yarn with such an inadequate toy.

00:15:31.269 --> 00:15:37.030
Turning her back on SCP-066, Josie the half-cat
searched for a way out of the room, and went

00:15:37.030 --> 00:15:40.690
back to prowling around the facility looking
for something to entertain herself.

00:15:40.690 --> 00:15:45.060
She noticed she was starting to get hungry;
maybe, if she got lucky, one of the researchers

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:46.870
would sneak her a piece of cheese.

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:53.589
Now go and check out “SCP-999: THE TICKLE
MONSTER” and “SCP-348 A GIFT FROM DAD”

00:15:53.589 --> 00:15:56.790
for more anomalies that are classed as Sweet,
Cute or Playful!

